program charging_years

-- ============================================================
-- Charging years: a period may not straddle the anniversary
-- ============================================================
--
-- Electricity distribution is billed under charging years that run
-- from 1 April to 31 March - the regulator publishes each year's
-- band rates for exactly that window. A billing run resolves a
-- supply's rates for ONE charging year, so a run whose period
-- crosses 1 April would price part of its consumption under the
-- wrong year's rates. The regulation's answer is simple: such a
-- period is not billable as one run. Split it at the boundary.
--
-- The trap is where that rule usually lives: as prevalidation in
-- application code, checked before the record is asked. A bug there
-- - or a caller that skips the check - and a straddling run is on
-- the books with no rule to refuse it. Here the boundary is the
-- record's own law. A date's charging year is a computed
-- coordinate: period_index counts how many whole years, anchored at
-- 1 April, have elapsed at that date. The run's gate demands the
-- period's two ends share that coordinate, the run records the
-- charging year the rules computed - the caller cannot state it - and
-- an invariant re-checks both over every committed run.
--
-- Two constants, honestly separated. The 1 APRIL is regulation -
-- the charging methodology fixes it. The YEAR 2000 is merely an
-- epoch: an arbitrary coordinate origin the index counts from, and
-- changing it would shift every index without moving a single
-- boundary. The recorded year below adds the epoch's year back, so
-- the record says 2026 - the charging year's own name - never a
-- bare offset from an origin nobody regulates.
const charging_epoch = (@2000-04-01)
const charging_epoch_year = (2000)

-- One billing run: the period it covers and the charging year it
-- resolved - computed by the rules, on the record, so a band-rate
-- lookup can name its year. The period is INCLUSIVE at both ends:
-- it covers starts_on through ends_on as billed days, so a full
-- charging year runs 1 April through 31 March. Runs are history:
-- one record per run, never rewritten.
predicate BillingRun(run: Subject, starts_on: Date, ends_on: Date, charging_year: Decimal)
    unique by (run)
    append only

-- The regulator publishes each year's band rates as one dated sheet,
-- and the sheet's own name for its year is the DAY IT BEGINS - "the
-- rates from 1 April 2026". The run above records the year as a
-- NUMBER. Two spellings of the same period, kept honest below: the
-- rules can turn a date into its year's index (period_index) and the
-- index back into the year's first day (period_start_of), so neither
-- spelling is ever taken on trust.
predicate RatesPublished(year_starts_on: Date)

-- Publishing is guarded by the round trip: take the date's year
-- index, compute that year's first day back, and demand it is the
-- same date. Only a true anniversary survives - a sheet claiming to
-- start on 3 April is refused, because year 26's first day is
-- 1 April 2026 and no other.
transformation publish_rates(year_starts_on):
    require the_date_is_an_anniversary: year_starts_on = period_start_of(charging_epoch, span(P1Y), period_index(charging_epoch, span(P1Y), year_starts_on))
    admit RatesPublished(year_starts_on)

-- A period runs forwards.
invariant runs_run_forwards:
    BillingRun(_, starts_on, ends_on, _) implies starts_on on_or_before ends_on

-- The regulation's rule, over every committed run: both ends of the
-- period sit in the same 1-April-anchored year. The coordinate is
-- computed inside the rule, each time - there is no stored boundary
-- date anyone maintains, and no prevalidation to skip.
invariant runs_stay_inside_one_charging_year:
    BillingRun(_, starts_on, ends_on, _)
    implies period_index(charging_epoch, span(P1Y), starts_on) = period_index(charging_epoch, span(P1Y), ends_on)

-- The recorded year is the record's own computation - the same
-- recompute pattern the metered-billing example prices with: the
-- caller never chooses the figure, and a figure that disagrees by
-- one cannot commit.
invariant runs_record_their_own_charging_year:
    BillingRun(_, starts_on, _, charging_year)
    implies charging_year = charging_epoch_year + period_index(charging_epoch, span(P1Y), starts_on)

-- A run may only price from rates that exist: its year's sheet must
-- be on the record. The rule reaches the sheet by computing the
-- year's first day from the recorded NUMBER - subtract the epoch's
-- year back off, ask for that period's start - so a run whose number
-- disagreed with the sheet it priced from could never commit. This
-- closes the gap the gate alone leaves open: the gate checks the
-- proposal in front of it, the invariant holds over every committed
-- run whatever order the record was built in.
invariant runs_price_from_a_published_year:
    BillingRun(_, _, _, charging_year)
    implies (exists year_starts_on: RatesPublished(year_starts_on) and year_starts_on = period_start_of(charging_epoch, span(P1Y), charging_year - charging_epoch_year))

-- The billing act. The gates refuse a straddling period and an
-- unpublished year before anything is admitted; the lets compute the
-- coordinate the run will carry and the year's first day to look the
-- rates up by. Note what the caller does NOT supply: the charging
-- year is not a parameter at all.
transformation open_run(run, starts_on, ends_on):
    require the_period_runs_forwards: starts_on on_or_before ends_on
    require the_period_stays_inside_one_charging_year: period_index(charging_epoch, span(P1Y), starts_on) = period_index(charging_epoch, span(P1Y), ends_on)
    let year_starts_on = period_start_of(charging_epoch, span(P1Y), period_index(charging_epoch, span(P1Y), starts_on))
    require the_years_rates_are_published: RatesPublished(year_starts_on)
    let charging_year = charging_epoch_year + period_index(charging_epoch, span(P1Y), starts_on)
    admit BillingRun(run, starts_on, ends_on, charging_year)
